        promptText: "In some countries, young people are encouraged to work or travel for a year between finishing high school and starting university studies. Discuss the advantages and disadvantages for young people who decide to do this.",
        modelAnswer: "Introduction:\nThe concept of a 'gap year'—taking a year off between high school and university to work or travel—has become increasingly popular among young adults in many Western nations. While this break from formal education can present certain challenges, I believe the personal growth and practical experience it offers make it an overwhelmingly advantageous pursuit.\n\nBody Paragraph 1:\nThere are several notable advantages to taking a gap year. Chief among them is the opportunity for immense personal development and broadening of horizons. Traveling internationally forces young people out of their comfort zones, teaching them independence, cultural empathy, and adaptability. These soft skills are invaluable and often difficult to acquire in a traditional classroom setting. Furthermore, choosing to work during this year provides crucial real-world experience. It allows school leavers to save money for their upcoming tuition fees while gaining practical skills and testing out potential career paths before committing to a specific, and expensive, university degree. This time spent reflecting often leads to a more focused and mature approach to their tertiary studies upon their return.\n\nBody Paragraph 2:\nConversely, there are undeniable disadvantages to delaying university. The most significant risk is a loss of academic momentum. After spending a year away from rigorous study and structured learning, some students find it incredibly difficult to re-adapt to the demands of academic writing and intensive studying. In extreme cases, the distraction of earning money or the allure of travel may cause individuals to abandon their plans for higher education entirely. Additionally, funding a year of travel can be prohibitively expensive, meaning this opportunity is often restricted to those from affluent backgrounds, thereby widening existing social inequalities.\n\nConclusion:\nIn conclusion, while a gap year carries the risk of disrupting academic momentum and can be financially demanding, the advantages are far more substantial. The independence gained through travel and the practical skills acquired through employment equip young adults with a level of maturity and focus that ultimately enhances their subsequent university experience and future career prospects.\n\nHow to Plan Your Answer:\n• Step 1: Analyze the Prompt: Identify the specific topic and the exact question type (e.g., Do you agree? Discuss both sides?).\n• Step 2: Formulate Your Stance: Decide on your clear overall opinion or thesis right away. (e.g., \"In conclusion, while a gap year carries the risk of disrupting academic momentum and can be financially demanding, the advantages are far more substantial.\")\n• Step 3: Outline Your Body Paragraphs: Pick one main supporting idea for each paragraph.\n  - Paragraph 1 Idea: There are several notable advantages to taking a gap year.\n  - Paragraph 2 Idea: Conversely, there are undeniable disadvantages to delaying university.\n• Step 4: Think of Examples: Before writing, quickly jot down one real-world example or logical reason to support each of your paragraph ideas.\n\n• Points to Note for this Question:\n  - Connecting to the Prompt: The prompt focuses on the issue: \"the central theme of the prompt\". The model answer tackles this by exploring \"There are several notable advantages to taking a gap year.\" in the first body paragraph and \"Conversely, there are undeniable disadvantages to delaying university.\" in the second.\n  - How to Come Up With This: To generate the body ideas yourself, always ask 'What is the strongest logical consequence of the prompt's theme?' This leads directly to the core thesis: while a gap year carries the risk of disrupting academic momentum and can be financially demanding, the advantages are far more substantial.\n  - Band 9 Strategy: Notice how the essay logically connects every supporting example back to the central theme of the prompt. This proves your position is completely consistent."
